  "Choose a plan for the question from the semantic layer. Reply with JSON only, e.g.\n{\"metric\": \"success_rate\", \"weeks\": [6, 7], \"filters\": {\"payment_method\": \"card\"}}\nweeks is [base week, current week] as integers 1-8 (wk8 is the latest); omit weeks when the question names no week.\nFor whether Settlements match transactions, use settlement_gap with one week, e.g.\n{\"metric\": \"settlement_gap\", \"weeks\": [5]}\nFor GMV, use gmv with one week; it is split by category, e.g. {\"metric\": \"gmv\", \"weeks\": [8]}\nfilters is optional: {dimension: value} when the question names one Segment, e.g. payment_method is upi, card, netbanking or wallet.\nMetrics: success_rate, settlement_gap, gmv\nFilter dimensions (success_rate only): payment_method, bank, bank_type, category, city, tier, merchant\nIf no metric above fits but these tables can answer it, reply instead with the one DuckDB statement the question asks for, e.g. {\"sql\": \"SELECT transactions.status, COUNT(*) AS n FROM transactions GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY n DESC\"}\nTables: transactions(txn_id, ts, merchant_id, bank_id, payment_method, amount, status, failure_reason); merchants(merchant_id, name, category, city, tier); banks(bank_id, name, type); settlements(merchant_id, date, settled_amount, settled_count). Join merchants and banks to transactions on merchant_id and bank_id; write full table names, never table aliases.\nOnly if the question is too vague for either, reply instead with {\"ask\": <one short question back to the user>}.\nQuestion: Why did success rate drop in week 6?": "{\"metric\": \"success_rate\", \"weeks\": [5, 6]}",
  "You are a payments analyst. Using only the computed headline and Facts below, reply with JSON only:\n{\"headline\": <one sentence>, \"hypotheses\": [<possible cause>], \"recommendations\": [<next step>]}\nQuestion: Why did success rate drop in week 6?\nComputed headline: Success rate fell 4.0pp from wk5 to wk6; 100% of it is a mix shift: traffic moved between payment methods, while rates within each payment method held.\nFacts:\n- Success rate wk5: 92.66%\n- Success rate wk6: 88.66%\n- card Success rate change: 0.00pp\n- netbanking Success rate change: -0.08pp\n- upi Success rate change: -0.01pp\n- wallet Success rate change: 0.09pp": "{\"headline\": \"Success rate fell 4.0pp from wk5 (92.66%) to wk6 (88.66%), and all of it is a mix shift: traffic moved toward payment methods that succeed less often, while each method's own rate held within 0.1pp.\", \"hypotheses\": [\"Wk6 is Diwali week: a festive sale may have pushed shoppers toward netbanking and wallets, which have lower success rates than UPI and cards.\", \"No payment method got worse, so a bank or gateway outage is unlikely to be the cause.\"], \"recommendations\": [\"Do not escalate to banks or gateway partners for wk6: rates within every payment method held.\", \"Confirm the dip reverses once the festive traffic ends.\", \"During sale weeks, report Success rate by payment method next to the overall rate, so a mix shift is not read as a reliability problem.\"]}",
